Multivariate metal-organic frameworks (MTV-MOFs), in which organic linkers have multiple functional groups, have been found to function in a manner where the whole performs better than the sum of the parts.1 The analogous chemistry involving the incorporation of multiple metals in a MOF structure is also being developed because mixing of metals is widely recognized as an essential feature to achieving optimal catalytic, optical and electronic properties, or even mimicking protein catalytic centers containing multiple metals.2 In general, both MTV-MOF systems suffer from the fact that the various organic functionalities lie on the same crystallographic position, and similarly for the case of metal ions. Thus, X-ray diffraction techniques offer limited information in deciphering the spatial arrangement of these components.3 In this report, we identified a MOF with a structure ideally suited for incorporating multivariate metal ions in both the organic linkers as well as the metal oxide secondary building units (SBUs) (Fig. 1A). The organic linker is tetrakis 1 $\dot{.}4$ -carboxyphenyl) porphyrin (TCPP) without $\begin{array}{r}{(\mathrm{TCPP-H}_{2})}\end{array}$ ) and with metals in the porphyrin unit of the linker, TCPP-Mg, TCPP-Co, TCPP-Ni, TCPP- $C\mathbf{u}$ , and TCPP-Zn. These were linked to $\mathbf{M}_{3}\mathbf{O}$ trigonal SBUs of various metals, $\bf{M g}_{3}\mathrm{{O}}$ , $\mathbf{Mn}_{3}\mathbf{O}$ , $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{\mathrm{i}}$ , $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}\mathrm{O}$ , and $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}$ including MTV metal SBUs, $\mathrm{Mn}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathrm{Fe}_{3^{-}\mathrm{x}}\mathrm{O}_{\mathrm{\bar{\cdot}}}$ $\mathrm{Ni_{x}F e_{3^{-x}}O}$ , $\mathrm{Co}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathrm{Ni}_{3^{-}\mathrm{x}}\mathrm{O}$ , $\mathrm{Mn}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathrm{Ni}_{3^{-}\mathrm{x}}\mathrm{O}$ and $\mathtt{M n_{x}M g_{3-x}O}$ to give the corresponding series of thirty-six MOFs with the general composition of $(\mathbf{M}_{3}{\bf O})_{2}(\mathbf{TCPP-M})_{3}$ (water molecules in SBU were deleted for clarity) (Fig. 1B). We show that for the series of $({\bf M}_{3}{\bf O})_{2}({\bf T C P P-M})_{3}$ in which each of the SBU and the TCPP have one kind of metal, the composition $\left(\mathbf{Mn}_{3}\mathbf{O}\right)_{2}(\mathbf{TCPP-Ni})_{3}$ has the highest conversion rate $(k_{o b s}=81.5{\times}10^{-2}\ \mathrm{h}^{-1})$ ) for the photo-oxidation of $^{1,5}$ -dihydroxynaphthalene as a test reaction.4 We also show that among the MTV-MOF series (mixed metals in the SBU), the composition $\mathrm{(Mn_{1.77}N i_{1.23}O)_{2}(T C P P}$ - ${\bf N i})_{3}$ exhibits further enhancement in reactivity $(k_{o b s}=1.03\mathrm{h}^{-1})$ towards the same reaction. More importantly, we have used spectroscopic techniques (XPS, and UV-vis DRS) to successfully decipher, for the first time, the spatial arrangement of metal ions in the MTV-MOF systems to distinguish two scenarios of mixing metals within and between SBUs. The impact of these two distinct mixed metal scenarios on the band gap of the MOF and the correlation to the reactivity of the system are also outlined.
Single component MOFs, $(\mathbf{M}_{3}{\bf O})_{2}(\mathbf{TCPP-M})_{3},$ are synthesized through mixing of the corresponding metal source with one of the organic linkers, $\mathrm{H_{4}T C P P-M}$ , acetic acid, and trifluoroacetic acid in DMF, and heating the mixture at $\mathbf{150\^{\circ}C}$ in glass vials for 12 to $36$ hours [Section $S_{3}$ , Supporting Information (SI)]. Various combinations of SBUs and organic linkers generated twenty-seven different single component MOFs, all with rod shaped crystals large enough for single crystal $\mathrm{x}$ -ray analysis. Results show that these MOFs are isoreticular with hexagonal topology (stp) and all crystalized into an identical space group of $P6/m m m$ (Fig. 1, S1).5 Each of the SBU has the general formula $\mathrm{{\bfM}}_{3}\mathrm{O}(\mathrm{COO})_{6}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{3},$ abbreviated as ${\bf M}_{3}\mathrm{O}$ $\mathbf{\dot{M}}=\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}$ , Mn, Fe, Co, Ni) to avoid the complexity in oxidation state of SBU-metals in the following text.6 In order to balance the charge in the formula, counter-anion/cation were added based on the elemental analysis results [Section S2, Supporting Information (SI)]. The Powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) patterns of the as-synthesized samples of these MOFs match well with the pattern simulated from single crystal structure data, indicating phase purity of the bulk samples (Fig.  S2-S6). The maintenance of the crystallinity of these activated MOFs is evidenced by the sharp peaks in their PXRD patterns (Fig. S21-S24).
MTV-MOFs composed of multiple metals are synthesized and activated under similar conditions as the single component MOFs but with mixed metal sources added to the reaction. The metal content of the resulting MTV-MOFs can be controlled through varying the stoichiometry of the starting materials (SI). Single crystal X-ray diffraction analysis of the MTV-MOFs shows that they have the same structure as the single component MOFs (Fig. 1B, SI). PXRD patterns of the bulk samples also confirm the phase purity of these MTVMOFs (Fig. S7-S12). The compositions of MTV-MOFs were quantified to be derived as $[\left(\mathbf{M_{1}}\right)_{3^{-\mathbf{X}}}(\mathbf{M}\mathbf{_{2}})_{\mathbf{x}}\mathbf{O}]_{\mathbf{2}}(\mathbf{TCPP-M})_{\mathbf{3}}$ . The detailed composition of each MTV-MOF is listed in the supporting information (Table $\mathrm{S}_{1}{-}\mathrm{S}_{3})$ ).
The permanent porosity of the guest-free MOFs is confirmed by the $\Nu_{2}$ adsorption measurements at $77\mathrm{K}$ (Fig. S35- $S_{42}\mathrm{\overline{{.}}}$ ). The calculated Brunauer-Emmett-Teller (BET) surface areas of selected single component MOFs, $(\mathbf{Ni_{3}O})_{2}$ (TCPP$\left.\mathbf{Cu}\right)_{3},$ $(\mathbf{Ni_{3}O})_{2}(\mathbf{TCPP-Co})_{3},$ $(\mathbf{Fe}_{3}\mathbf{O})_{2}(\mathbf{TCPP-Co})_{3}$ and MTVMOF, $\left(\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}_{1.77}\mathbf{Ni}_{1.23}\mathbf{O}\right)_{2}\left(\mathbf{TCPP-Ni}\right)_{3}$ are 2200, 2090, 1660 and $1380{\mathrm{~m}}^{2}{\mathrm{g}}^{-1}$ , respectively. The $\Nu_{2}$ isotherms of these MOFs are type IV, which is typical for mesoporous materials, and the starting point of the second step is observed at $P/P_{\mathrm{o}}=0.20$ , corresponding to a pore size of $3.2\ \mathrm{nm}$ , in good agreement with their single crystal structure (Figure S1, S14, S15). Several MOFs exhibit limited $\Nu_{2}$ adsorption upon guest removal, however, their SBU-linker connections were conserved. Synchrotron X-ray absorption analysis of these MOFs reveals that the coordination environment of metals was maintained as reflected in the good fit between the experimental data and the profile simulated from the single crystal structure of the MOFs (Figure $S_{56-S68}\mathrm{\cdot}$ ).7
Although several MTV-MOF systems with multivariate metals have been reported, the specific arrangement of metals throughout the crystal structure remains unknown.8 It is difficult because X-ray diffraction analysis provides limited distinction between metal atoms of similar sizes. This becomes even more challenging when their crystallographic position is the same. There are two scenarios for the spatial arrangements of metals in a MTV-MOF composed of two SBU metals: (1) the domain arrangement, where two different SBUs, each of which is composed of three metal atoms of the same kind, are mixed to form domains in the MOF structure (Fig. 2A), and (2) the well-mixed arrangement, where there are two kind of metals in the same SBU and these mixed metal SBUs are present throughout the entire MOF crystal (Fig. 2B).
Prior to deciphering these two scenarios for metal arrangements in MTV-MOFs, we need to make sure that the MTV-MOF is not a heterogeneous physical mixture of MOF crystals of different metals. In order to rule out this possibility, we applied energy dispersive spectra (EDS) to map the presence of various metals using scanning electron microscopy (SEM). Two MTV-MOFs, $\left(\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}_{1.45}\mathbf{Fe}_{1.55}\mathbf{O}\right)_{2}\left(\mathbf{TCPP-Ni}\right)_{3}$ and $(\mathrm{Ni}_{_{2.{\bf o}7}}\mathrm{Fe}_{{\bf o}.{\bf g}_{3}}{\bf O})_{2}(\mathrm{TCPP-Co})_{3}.$ are used here as illustrative examples. For the first MTV-MOF, if it was a physical mixture of $\left(\mathbf{Mn}_{3}\mathbf{O}\right)_{2}(\mathbf{TCPP-Ni})_{3}$ and $(\mathbf{Fe_{3}O})_{\scriptscriptstyle2}(\mathbf{TCPP-Ni})_{\scriptscriptstyle3},$ we would expect to observe the presence of Mn and Fe separately in their corresponding crystal specimens. However, the EDS mapping shows that both Mn and Fe are distributed homogenously within all crystals of the sample (Fig. 2C). This clearly indicates that this MTV-MOF is not a physical mixture. Similar observations were made for samples of $(\mathrm{Ni}_{2.{\bf o}7}\mathrm{Fe}_{{\bf o}.{\bf g}3}{\bf O})_{2}(\mathrm{TCPP-Co})_{3}$ (Fig. 2D).
