However, due to the resolution limit of EDS in SEM,9 EDS mapping is not sufficiently accurate to differentiate the two scenarios of MTV-MOFs at the atomic scale. Thus, we turned to XPS to study the coordination environment of the metals in the SBUs. If there are two kinds of metals in the same SBU, the coordination environments of the metals of each kind will be influenced by the presence of adjacent ones of different kinds. These changes can be detected by XPS. In the domain scenario, the interaction between two kinds of metals is negligible because they are separated by the linkers. XPS data for $\left(\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n_{1.45}F e_{1.55}O}\right)_{2}\left(\mathbf{TCPP-Ni}\right)_{3}$ gives almost identical binding energies for $\mathrm{Mn}^{2+}$ and $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ as their single component counterparts, $\left(\mathbf{Mn}_{3}\mathbf{O}\right)_{2}(\mathbf{TCPP-Ni})_{3}$ and $(\mathbf{Fe}_{3}{\bf O})_{\scriptscriptstyle2}(\mathbf{TCPP-Ni})_{\scriptscriptstyle3},$ respectively (Fig. 2E and G). Thus, the Mn and Fe metals are in separate SBUs for $\left(\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}_{1.45}\mathbf{Fe}_{1.55}\mathbf{O}\right)_{2}\left(\mathbf{TCPP-Ni}\right)_{3},$ and the spatial arrangements of metals follows the domain scenario. In contrast, significant shifts in binding energy are observed in the case of $(\mathrm{Ni}_{2..07}\mathrm{Fe}_{{\bf o}.93}{\bf O})_{2}(\mathrm{TCPP-Co})_{3}$ in comparison. to the single component MOFs, $\left(\mathbf{Ni_{3}O}\right)_{2}(\mathbf{TCPP-Co})_{3}$ and $(\mathbf{Fe}_{3}\mathbf{O})_{2}(\mathbf{TCPP-Co})_{3}$ (Fig. 2F and H). The Ni $\mathsf{2}\mathsf{p}_{2/3}$ peak shifted by ${\bf0.2e V}$ and the Fe $\scriptstyle2\mathrm{P}_{2/3}$ peak shifted by $\mathbf{o.}5\mathrm{eV}$ , respectively. These results prove that the Fe and Ni metals are in the same SBUs for the MTV-MOF $(\mathrm{Ni}_{_{2.{\bf0}7}}\mathrm{Fe}_{{\bf0}.{\bf9}3}{\bf O})_{2}(\mathrm{TCPP-Co})_{3},$ adopting the well-mixed scenario. In order to rule out the influence from oxidation states on the determination of metal spatial arrangement, a combination of spectroscopic techniques were applied including XANES, Mössbauer and EPR (SI). The results clearly confirm that each of the metals in the SBU has only one single oxidation state in both well-mixed and domain spatial arrangements. Specifically, Mn, Co, Ni and Fe exhibit the oxidation state of $+2$ , $+2$ , $+2$ and $+3$ , respectively. The existence of two scenarios of metal arrangement in MTV-MOFs may originate from differences in the ionic radius of the metals and their affinity to coordinate with oxygen.10 Given the ionic radius $(\mathrm{Mn}^{2\dot{+}}\ \mathrm{o}.97\ \mathring{\mathrm{A}},\ \mathrm{Fe}^{3^{+}}\ \mathrm{o}.79\ \mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ , and $\mathbf{\check{N}i}^{2+}~\mathbf{o}.83~\mathring{\mathrm{A}};$ ,11 and the electronegativity $(\mathrm{Mn\1.55}$ , Fe $\mathbf{1.83}$ , Ni 1.91),12 the similarity between $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ and $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ allows them to be compatible to present in the same SBU and form the well-mixed arrangement; while the significant difference between $\mathrm{Mn}^{2+}$ and $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ leads to aggregation to form the domain arrangement.
Noting that the band structure might be altered with the mixing of metals,2d we sought to test the catalytic performance of MTV-MOFs having domain or well-mixed metal arrangement. Here, we used a common photocatalytic reaction to test the impact of various band structures of these MTV-MOFs and to correlate their reactivity with the kind and ratio of various metals present in their SBUs. This also provides further comparisons to elucidate the different behavior exhibited by the two spatial arrangements. Photooxidation of 1,5-dihydroxynaphthalene (DHN) was used as a model reaction with the MOFs being the catalysts. Upon light absorption, singlet oxygen is generated by the electron excited from the MOFs as illustrated by $\left(\mathbf{Mn}_{3}\mathbf{O}\right)_{2}(\mathbf{TCPP-Ni})_{3}$ (Fig. 3A), then this singlet oxygen react with DHN to produce $5$ -hydroxynaphthalene- $^{\cdot1,4}$ -dione (Juglone). By monitoring the concentration of DHN and Juglone in solution using their finger-print in the UV-vis spectra, the kinetics of this reaction can be revealed (Fig. 3B). The effects of metals in the SBU and organic linkers in the photo-reaction kinetics were systematically investigated in single component MOFs. Their conversion rates $(k_{o b s})$ range from $42.9$ to $81.5\times10^{-2}~\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ in the order of TCPP- $\mathrm{\Delta\sqrt{i}>}$ TCPP-Co $>$ TCPP-Mg >TCPP-Cu > TCPP- $\cdot\mathrm{H}_{2}$ keeping the metals in the SBU constant. Similarly, varying the metals in SBU leads to different $k_{o b s}$ ranging from 39.5 to $81.5\times10^{-2}~\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ in the order of $\mathrm{Mn}_{3}\mathrm{O}>\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}>\mathrm{Ni}_{3}\mathrm{O}>$ $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}>\mathrm{Mg}_{3}\mathrm{O}$ , keeping the organic linker composition constant. It is worth noting that the catalytic performance of all these MOFs are significantly higher than the discrete unlinked porphyrin linker, TCPP-Co, which only exhibits a $k_{o b s}$ of $18.5\times10^{-2}\mathrm{~h~}^{-1}$ . The optimum combination in this MOF series is that in $\left(\mathbf{Mn_{3}O}\right)_{2}(\mathbf{TCPP-Ni})_{3}$ , with the $k_{o b s}$ of $81.5\times{10}^{-2}$ $\mathbf{h}^{-1}$ , which is $330\%$ better than the TCPP linker. Furthermore, synchrotron X-ray absorption spectra (XAS) of MOFs before and after catalysis match closely, indicating the stability of their structure during catalysis (Fig. $\mathrm{{S58–}}$ S68).
Next, we examined the photocatalytic performance of MTV-MOFs having the two different spatial arrangements of metals. MTV-MOFs of the domain spatial arrangement composed of $\mathrm{Mn}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathrm{Fe}_{3^{-}\mathrm{x}}\mathrm{O}$ , $\mathrm{{Mn}_{x}\mathrm{{Co}_{3^{-x}}\mathrm{{O}}}}$ and $\mathtt{M n_{x}M g_{3-x}O}$ SBUs, exhibit catalytic rates falling in between their corresponding MOFs with single component SBU metal (Fig. $3^{\mathsf{C})}$ ). In the case of MTV-MOFs of the well-mixed spatial arrangement composed of $\mathrm{Mn}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathrm{Ni}_{3^{-}\mathrm{x}}\mathrm{O}$ and $\mathrm{Ni_{x}F e_{3^{-x}}O}$ , their conversion rates exceed the best of their corresponding single component MOFs, and $\left(\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}_{1.77}\mathbf{Ni}_{1.23}\mathbf{O}\right)_{2}\left(\mathbf{TCPP-Ni}\right)_{3}$ exceeds all the MTV-MOFs with domains (Fig. 3D). Specifically, $\left(\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}_{1.77}\mathbf{Ni}_{1.23}\mathbf{O}\right)_{2}\left(\mathbf{TCPP-Ni}\right)_{3}$ shows a further $26\%$ increase in the catalytic performance $(k_{o b s}=1.03\ \mathrm{h}^{-1})$ ) in comparison to its best single component MOF counterpart $\left(\mathbf{Mn}_{3}\mathbf{O}\right)_{2}(\mathbf{TCPP-Ni})_{3}$ . $(\mathrm{Ni}_{_{2.{\bf o}7}}\mathrm{Fe}_{{\bf o}.{\bf o}3}{\bf O})_{2}(\mathrm{TCPP-Co})_{3}$ shows a conversion rate of $k_{o b s}=$ $7^{8.3}\times10^{-2}\mathrm{~h}^{-1}$ , $54\%$ better than its best single component MOF counterpart $(\bf N i_{3}O)_{2}(T C P P-C o)_{3}$ $(k_{o b s}=50.8\times10^{-2}~\mathrm{h^{-1}})$ . The control experiment using the physical mixture of $50\%$ $(\mathbf{Fe_{3}O})_{\scriptscriptstyle2}(\mathbf{TCPP-Co})_{\scriptscriptstyle3}$ and $50\%\ \left(\bf N i_{3}O\right)_{2}(T C P P-C o)_{3}$ shows as expected conversion rate between the value of pure $(\mathbf{Fe}_{3}\mathbf{O})_{\scriptscriptstyle2}(\mathbf{TCPP-Co})_{\scriptscriptstyle3}$ and pure $\left({\bf N i_{3}O}\right)_{2}\left({\bf T C P P-C o}\right)_{3},$ , and significantly lower than well-mixed MTV-MOFs of the same metal component. Indeed, this clearly shows that the well-mixed spatial arrangement within the SBUs leads to better performance than the sum of the parts. The best conversion rate in this MTV-MOF series was found in $(\mathbf{Ni_{2.o7}F e_{o.o.o3}O})_{2}$ (TCPP$\mathbf{Co})_{3}$ with Ni/Fe ratio close to 2:1, which corresponds to two $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ ions and one $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ ion in each well-mixed metal SBU.
In order to uncover the reason for the better catalytic performance observed for mixed metal MTV-MOFs, we obtain the band structure of these photo-catalysts using UV-vis DRS to determine the absolute band gap in combination with valence band-XPS (VB-XPS) to reveal the precise position of the valence band (Fig. $3^{\mathrm{E}}$ and F, SI). We found that when the SBUs of different metals were aggregated in domains, the corresponding band structure is very close to the single component MOF. On the other hand, when the different metals are present in the same SBU and are well-mixed, their band gaps change dramatically. For example, in the case of $(\mathrm{Ni}_{2..07}\mathrm{Fe}_{{\bf o}.93}{\bf O})_{2}(\mathrm{TCPP-Co})_{3},$ its band gap shows better overlap with that of the singlet oxygen, which favors electron/hole transfer to oxygen leading to accelerated reaction rate (Fig. $\mathrm{_{3,F}}\mathrm{_{,}}$ ).14 Although domain MTV-MOF $\left(\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}_{1.45}\mathbf{Fe}_{1.55}\mathbf{O}\right)_{2}\left(\mathbf{TCPP-Ni}\right)_{3}$ shows negligible difference with its corresponding single SBU metal MOFs, $\left(\mathbf{Mn_{3}O}\right)_{2}$ (TCPP${\bf N i})_{3}$ and $(\mathbf{Fe_{3}O})_{\scriptscriptstyle2}(\mathbf{TCPP-Ni})_{\scriptscriptstyle3},$ in energy level structures, the differences in their conversion rate can be observed. This is attributed to differences in their light absorption ability (Fig. $3^{\mathrm{E}}$ and $\mathsf{S79}$ ).
In conclusion, we have been able to decipher the spatial arrangement in multi-metal MTV-MOFs and find that the metals are distributed in either a domain or in well-mixed spatial arrangements. The latter scenario profoundly influences the reactivity of the MOF as evidenced by the reaction rates and as supported by their band gap.
